# homma-api

The vocabulary every other crate speaks. Types and the traits over them, and no
I/O of any kind.

## A record

Everything homma stores is a record. A message, a task, a delivery cursor, an
entry in a monitoring log: one shape, distinguished by what its kind says about
it.

Three fields are required of every record, because homma's own machinery reads
them.

**`id`** addresses it. **`kind`** names the declaration that governs it.
**`provenance`** is a reference to whoever created it, and the standing of a
record is derived from what that reference points at rather than stored beside it:
a person's own reference is ratified, an agent's is agent output, a forge's is
mapped and read-only. Storing the standing as well would let the two disagree,
which is the whole reason it is derived.

Everything else a record carries is declared by its kind.

## A kind

A kind is configuration, not code. It names the attributes a record of that kind
has, their types, which are required, and whether records of that kind may be
edited after they are written.

That last flag is what lets correspondence and structural work share one engine.
Messages declare themselves append-only and are never edited; tasks declare
themselves mutable and change as work moves. Nothing about the store knows which
is which, and a workspace that wants a third behaviour declares a third kind.

## Attribute types

Present, because something uses each:

**`text`**, with a hint saying whether it is plain or carries markup.
**`instant`**, a point in time. **`ref<ns>`**, a reference into a namespace, which
is the one type that collapses several: a handle is a reference into the
registry's namespace, a link to another record is a reference into that record's
kind, and a forge reference is a reference into a forge's.

Deliberately absent, and each for a reason rather than an oversight: a date
distinct from an instant, a duration, a number, a decimal with declared precision,
a recurrence, a fractional rank, and an ordered workflow with terminal and
off-path states. Each is designed and none is needed by the first records that
exist. Kinds being configuration means every one of them is additive when it
arrives, so building them ahead of a consumer would fix a shape nothing has
pushed on.

## Configuration

One required key names the content repository. Everything else has a default
homma will create and an override for a workspace whose convention differs.
Identities are blocks: a role, a handle, and whatever that role needs. A person
needs a git identity and a workspace; a consultant needs neither.

## Mapped and staffed

An identity whose role owns a workspace is in one of two states, and they are not
degrees of completeness.

**Staffed** means it has a workspace on disk, a git identity, and repositories. It
is somebody who works.

**Mapped** means the entry records that a boundary has been drawn and who owns it,
so the same ground is not carved twice by somebody arriving later. It has no
workspace and is not supposed to have one.

Without the distinction the two are indistinguishable, because a mapped entry
lacks exactly the fields an unfinished one lacks. A registry holding a dozen
mapped entries then reports a dozen broken ones, and a report that is wrong a
dozen times is a report nobody reads.

**The staffing state is declared, never inferred from an absent workspace.** Inference is
cheaper and wrong: an entry whose workspace was mistyped or dropped would read as
a deliberate decision, which is the confusion the distinction exists to remove. So
an entry says it is staffed, and a staffed entry lacking its workspace is still a
gap and still reported.
